Written because each has recurred and each was described in chat twice without
being recorded. An idea not written down is an idea that will be forgotten, and
this is the second hourly check where I said "I'll note this next turn".

exp6753 does not fail, it STALLS. Measured at 3h50m wall time: 35.21 CPU-seconds
total and unchanged across a full hour, state S, wchan __x64_sys_poll.cold, with
a child owning a llama-server resident at 17,884 MiB on GPU 0. The driver is
blocked in poll waiting on a response that never arrives while its server sits
loaded and idle.

The conductor reproduced it independently: "Live object-table fetch-on-demand A/B
v2" failed at 03:55Z and 05:18Z, both on the 4803s wall-clock cap. A hand-launched
run and a conductor task, different harnesses, same experiment, both stuck — so
this is a property of the work, not of who launched it, and the earlier
"wall-clock cap" reading of exp6753's history was a symptom rather than a cause.

A correction is filed with it: at 03:35Z I reported this run as "computing"
because the GPUs were at 98-99%. That load belonged to other processes. Liveness
is not progress and GPU utilisation is not attribution.

Second observation: long-running GPU jobs here go log-silent while alive, twice
now. The holdout A/B stopped writing its runner log at 13:57 while its shard kept
growing until 19:21 — six hours understated. exp6753 has written nothing since
its launch minute.

Whether the two share a mechanism is UNKNOWN, and the entry says so. A severed
handle, nohup buffering, and a stall with nothing to log look identical from
outside. Three long-running measurements have also been killed by an unidentified
exit=143, and the entry explicitly refuses to merge that into one story without
evidence — that is how a plausible cause becomes recorded fact, which this project
has done twice this week.

Queued three tasks, the first being to py-spy or strace the LIVE pid before
killing it, since the stall is reproducible and the evidence is in memory now.

